  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The remnants of Hurricane Isaias will bring gusty \ndownsloping winds off the Chautauqua Ridge and then a soaking rain \nto the region tonight through Monday morning.\n\nLatest satellite imagery shows the remnants of Hurricane Isaias \nmoving north across the Tennessee valley and approaching the Ohio \nvalley region. High clouds have spread across western NY today \nassociated with this storm, but any rainfall spreading across our \nregion will hold off until Sunday morning.\n\nBeforehand, surface pressure gradients will begin to increase this \nevening as high pressure exits off the New England coast. This will \nsupport increased southeasterly flow across the region with 850mb \nwinds around 30-35 kt. Forecast soundings across the Chautauqua \nRidge in southwestern NY highlight a low-level inversion supporting \ndownsloping flow and elevated winds starting this evening. The sharp \ninversion will remain in-place up until the arrival of moisture from \nthe south Sunday morning that will begin to cool the low-level \ntemperature profile. NBM probability of wind gusts exceeding 35 mph \nacross the Chautauqua Ridge maximizes between 10 PM tonight and 4 AM \nSunday morning. \n\nWhile an isolated shower may be possible across the Southern Tier \nafter midnight, chances for precipitation will increase through \nSunday morning, especially after 8 AM, across western NY. Synoptic \nscale forcing with the post-tropical system will spread over the \nregion promoting the continuation of widespread rain through much of \nthe day. One uncertainty that remains is the strength of the larger \nscale forcing as there is a handoff between the vertically stacked \nlow over the Ohio valley and the development of the new surface\nlow off the Mid-Atlantic coast. Regardless, expecting \nprecipitation totals between 0.25\" and 0.75\" with Sunday's \nrainfall, especially from the Southern Tier through the Finger \nLakes region. Areas across western NY will be on the lower side \nof that amount as latest trends in hi- res models (HRRR/REFS) \ndrift the higher QPF totals farther east. Rainfall will begin to\ntaper off from west to east overnight into early Monday morning\nas the upper level disturbance begins to shift east across the \nMid-Atlantic.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The next round of rain showers arrives late Monday \nnight into Tuesday with a cold frontal passage across western NY. \n\nA low pressure system will move west to east across the Great Lakes \nlate Monday into Tuesday with rain showers along a leading shortwave \nas well as ahead of the cold frontal and surface low passage. At \nthis time, a couple isolated thunderstorms embedded with rain \nshowers cannot be ruled out. Mid-level lapse rates look to be near 7 \nC/km, but storms will likely be elevated with poor low-level lapse \nrates. There remains quite a bit of uncertainty with high \ntemperatures across the region Tuesday, mainly tied to the timing of \nprecipitation and the frontal passage. While the NBM shows afternoon \nhighs in the low 70s, the 25th and 75th percentile values from input \nguidance provides a range between the low 60s to mid 70s for much of \nwestern NY.",
